  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nMain forecast focus today is the continuation of abnormal heat \nthanks to a broad upper-level ridge allowing a warm airmass into the \nregion. Over central and north central South Dakota specifically, \nheat index values will top out over 100 degrees this afternoon, and \nHeat Risk will reach the Major category in some places. With these \nfactors in mind, a Heat Advisory has been issued for this afternoon \nover central and north central South Dakota. Admittedly parts of the \narea under the advisory are a bit borderline and/or spotty for \nreaching Heat Advisory criteria of 100 degree apparent temperatures. \nOne potential failure point for temperatures today will be the \naddition of smoke across the region originating from wildfires in \nthe Pacific Northwest. Smoke is mainly expected to remain aloft \n(still having an impact on temperatures), although some near-surface \nsmoke remains possible. Concentrations at the surface may cause some \nminor visibility  reductions and push air quality up to Moderate \nlevels (which corresponds to Air Quality Index values of 50-100). \nThis level of air quality means that mainly those sensitive to smoke \nwill be impacted. \n\nBeginning this evening into tonight, a severe weather threat will \nemerge over parts of north central South Dakota, mainly west of the \nMissouri River. The latest high-resolution guidance keeps most of \nthe storms to the west until the late evening to early overnight \nhours. By the time storms reach the Aberdeen CWA, they will \nencounter an environment that will not be particularly supportive of \nstorms. A lack of strong shear (<30 knots) and CAPE of around 1000 \nJ/kg will likely struggle to maintain storms, and storms that make \nit may dissipate shortly after crossing the CWA border. Naturally \nwith collapsing storms, wind will be the main severe threat. DCAPE \nvalues in particular will be quite high, with high-res model \nguidance resolving upwards of 1500 J/kg over the area. Fairly strong \nlow-level lapse rates (Near-dry adiabatic lapse rates all the way up \nto roughly 5-6 km in model soundings) may also be present, allowing \nfor easy access to the surface. Diurnal cooling may temper these \nstrong lapse rates depending on when storms arrive over the area, \nbut the threat likely remains. A Marginal Risk is in place to cover \nthis threat. \n\nShowers and storms will continue from Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning, spreading eastward throughout the morning hours. While \nsevere weather during that morning period can't entirely be ruled \nout, chances for severe weather increase in the Thursday afternoon \nto evening period. Wind will once again be a threat, although \nperhaps under a less impressive DCAPE environment than on Wednesday. \nThe persistent cloud cover will also limit the diurnal heating and \nsubsequently low-level lapse rates. Hail is also a possible threat, \nbut storms will again be dealing with shear up to roughly 30 knots, \nwhich may make it difficult. MUCAPE values do reach around 2000 J/kg \nhowever, so that will be one thing working in favor of storm \ndevelopment and maintenance. Another Marginal Risk is in place \nThursday, this time covering the entire Aberdeen CWA. \n\nAs the shortwave influence exits the region, it will begin to deepen \nover the eastern CONUS. The upper-level ridge will therefore become \nmore positively tilted, still allowing for a warm airmass to sit \noverhead. The presence of the trough to the east may create a bit of \na gradient in temperatures over the forecast region however. This \nwill likely result in near-normal temperatures (mid-80s) over \nnortheastern South Dakota and western Minnesota and above normal \ntemperatures (90s) over central and north central South Dakota. \nEnsembles then show the potential for an upper-level trough to move \nthrough early next week, with the base of the trough located over \nthe Northern Plains. This may provide the next chances for rain over \nthe area, although at this time range it is difficult to pinpoint \nexactly. Currently confidence is highest in precipitation to the \nnorth of the CWA in North Dakota based on the location of the jet \nstreak in ensemble forecasts, but it will likely dip far enough \nsouth to support some development south of the state line as well.",
